    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2617a726_4d312603",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 12,
      "author": {
        "id": 1874590
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-02-20T22:52:07Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Hi Eric,\nIn AAOS (Android for automotive) kernel lockdown implementation prevents CarPowerManagementService from triggering transition to suspend(ram, disk). Suspend work in Android that doesn\u0027t use kernel 5.15, however 5.15 is used by automotive partners for Android 13. To unblock suspend functionality for automotive Android this patch need to be backported to kernel 5.15.",
      "parentUuid": "0db78bf0_208188a2",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 9,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 12,
        "endChar": 60
      },
      "revId": "ddab7367826ada36a0cb67360948ad402019f27a",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "9b55a5df_b5942a1e",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 12,
      "author": {
        "id": 1137063
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-02-20T23:02:11Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "That issue occurs because Android is denying the \"integrity\" permission, right?  If so, can\u0027t the issue be fixed either by updating Android to stop denying the \"integrity\" permission, *or* by backporting this upstream patch that makes the \"integrity\" permission stop being enforced?  It seems that a decision was made to use the second solution instead of the first --- why?  If this security policy is really ineffective, how come it hasn\u0027t been removed from Android yet?",
      "parentUuid": "2617a726_4d312603",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 9,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 12,
        "endChar": 60
      },
      "revId": "ddab7367826ada36a0cb67360948ad402019f27a",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "e60ac6d7_b61c0ce2",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 12,
      "author": {
        "id": 1561651
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-02-20T23:53:36Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "That\u0027s right. The reason is to avoid the spread of lockdown rules in vendors\u0027 policies. For the AAOS case, if we removed the neverallow now, OEMs will start adding allow rules. This will make any further clean up more tedious down the road.\n\nSince this access vector (lockdown) was found not appropriate for SELinux and is not available in the current kernel version, we know that it needs to be removed so let\u0027s stop it early.\n\nBut I agree, once it is backported and we don\u0027t have any supported kernel relying on that rule (I believe 5.15 is the only branch), then we can remove the neverallow from the policy.",
      "parentUuid": "9b55a5df_b5942a1e",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 9,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 12,
        "endChar": 60
      },
      "revId": "ddab7367826ada36a0cb67360948ad402019f27a",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ffe05001_fb4a458b",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 12,
      "author": {
        "id": 1137063
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-02-21T03:53:38Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Android\u0027s SELinux policy already allows confidentiality to everyone:\n\n```\nallow domain self:lockdown confidentiality;\n```\n\nHowever, it does *not* allow integrity to everyone.  If we believe that integrity should be allowed to everyone too, shouldn\u0027t we add:\n\n```\nallow domain self:lockdown integrity;\n```\n\nI can see how backporting this kernel patch *in addition to that* may make sense, as it may allow these rules to be entirely removed more quickly than otherwise would have been the case.  What\u0027s confusing to me is that kernel patch, which was intentionally not backported to LTS, is being presented *on its own* as the solution when Android\u0027s SELinux policy is still denying integrity.  If the policy were to be updated first, then you could point to the policy always allowing all the lockdown permissions including integrity, and this backport would make a lot more sense.",
      "parentUuid": "e60ac6d7_b61c0ce2",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 9,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 12,
        "endChar": 60
      },
      "revId": "ddab7367826ada36a0cb67360948ad402019f27a",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
